# Files a GENERATOR owns outright — never hand-edited, always rewritten whole.
#
# Bean `folio-assistant-1swy`. Measured 2026-09-24 over three days:
#
#   106 commits  cat-harness/docs/cat-harness/auto-docs/index/index.html
#    45 commits  cat-harness/docs/glossary/index.md            (1.5 MB)
#    18 commits  cat-harness/test/results/audit-coverage.qa-results.json
#
# Merging one branch cost THREE rounds on one of them in a single sitting. Every
# concurrent pull request pays a resolution round on a file that is regenerated
# from the whole corpus anyway, and every review carries a 1.5 MB diff nobody
# reads.
#
# ## Two attributes, both portable — no `git config` required
#
# `linguist-generated=true`  GitHub collapses it in review, so the diff stops
#                            burying the change somebody is actually reviewing.
# `-diff -merge`             git stops attempting a line-by-line merge on a file
#                            no human edits. The conflict is then ONE clean
#                            conflict to resolve by regenerating, rather than
#                            markers buried inside a megabyte.
#
# A merge driver that auto-resolved would need `git config merge.*.driver`, which
# is per-checkout: it would work here and nowhere else, including CI. Deliberately
# not that.
#
# ## Why this is safe, and it rests on a checked fact rather than on care
#
# All three are gated in CI — `check:glossary`, `auto:docs:check` and
# `audit:coverage:require-all`. A resolution that takes the wrong side therefore
# CANNOT SHIP: it reddens, and the repair is one regeneration command. Without
# those gates this entry would be a way to lose work quietly.
#
# ## What is deliberately NOT here, and it is the whole discipline
#
# **Not `cat-harness/test/results/**`**, though 840 generated files live there.
# `kg-audit` reads its prior judgements back — `readAttestations` — so the
# file holding them carries adjudications that an earlier run or a person
# recorded. Since bean `2gst` that is `test/attestations/**`, not the `kg-qa`
# sidecar; neither is marked here. Such a file is not purely generated, and
# `-merge` on one would discard an attestation with nothing said. `audit-coverage.qa-results.json` is named
# individually for exactly that reason: its producer reads the committed file
# only to compare for staleness, and writes a fresh document every time.
#
# So the test of whether a file belongs here is NOT "is it generated" but
# **"does its producer carry anything forward from the existing file"**. A glob
# over a generated directory is how the wrong answer gets in.
# `scripts/tests/gitattributes.test.ts` holds that line.

cat-harness/docs/glossary/index.md                      linguist-generated=true -diff -merge
cat-harness/docs/cat-harness/auto-docs/**               linguist-generated=true -diff -merge
cat-harness/test/results/audit-coverage.qa-results.json linguist-generated=true -diff -merge
cat-harness/test/results/tool-runs/lsi-index/cat-harness/skills.tool-run.json linguist-generated=true -diff -merge

# Line endings the interpreters require, whatever core.autocrlf says.
# bash chokes on CRLF; cmd.exe mis-parses some LF-only batch files. Both are
# stored LF in the repo and checked out as the interpreter wants them, so
# cat-harness/scripts/gen-bat-wrappers.sh --check compares like with like on every OS.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# ## Added 2026-10-01, and TWO candidates were refused in the same pass
#
# `skills.tool-run.json` qualifies on all three counts. Its producer
# (`writeToolRun`, `schemas/tool-run.ts`) composes the body from its argument
# and reads the existing file only as `readFileSync(p) === body`, to skip a
# pointless write — nothing is carried forward. And `lsi:skills:check`
# REDDENS on a wrong resolution: measured by corrupting `inputFingerprint`,
# which took the gate from exit 0 to exit 1.
#
# Refused, each by the same measurement rather than by reading:
#
#   · `skill-register.qa-results.json` — nothing catches a wrong resolution.
#     Corrupted its `producer.script_hash` and a family summary:
#     `skill:register:check` exited 0, and 66 tests across
#     `skill-register.test.ts` + `qa-results.test.ts` passed.
#   · `skills.lsi.json` — same result. Corrupted its `fingerprint` and
#     `lsi:skills:check` exited 0; that gate validates the RUN RECORD's
#     fingerprint, never the sidecar's own contents.
#
# Both pass the carry-forward test and fail the gate test, which is the half
# that makes an entry safe rather than clever. Bean `eqxp`.
#
# ## What this buys, stated honestly, because the first write-up got it wrong
#
# `-merge` does NOT reduce the NUMBER of conflicts. Measured in a scratch
# repository: with `gen.json -merge`, a merge where both sides changed the
# file still exits 1 with the path unresolved; git declares a conflict and
# leaves OURS as the tentative result. What it buys is what `oxka` claimed and
# no more — one clean whole-file conflict to resolve by regenerating, instead
# of markers buried inside a generated blob, plus a collapsed diff in review.
# Bean `eqxp` carried the stronger claim (that an entry would have cut a real
# merge from five conflicts to two); that claim is false and is corrected
# there. Removing these conflicts, rather than tidying them, needs the files
# off `main` altogether.
